YAFAP

This is my first post on a newsgroup, so please let me know if it is
in the right place.

After playing Nethack for 15 years and hundreds (thousands?) of tries
I finally beat it and now it's time to make my Yet Another First
Ascension Post (YAFAP).

Warning - Spoilers below!

A Good Start

I am Scottvalas, a typical elf who was enamored with a beautiful elf
enchantress. But unfortunately, I was ugly as hell (Charisma 7) and
the enchantress would have nothing to do with me! In despair I joined
the priesthood of Set, hoping I could mend the hole in my heart
through spiritual enlightenment (I had a starting wisdom of 20!). My
other stats were average.

I eventually decided that the only way to woo the enchantress was to
acquire the fabled Amulet of Yendor, as legend had it the Amulet could
give its owner whatever he desired, perhaps even immortality itself!

My expedition started off quite well as the first few dungeon levels
yielded several large shops. After finding a potion of gain ability, I
decided to bless it to increase all of my mediocre stats instead of
just one. However, upon quaffing the now-blessed smoky potion a djinni
emerged and granted a wish!

A few seconds later I was outfitted with sleek new gray dragon scale
mail! Even better, one of the shops had gauntlets of power for sale.
"I've got great armor and now great strength," I thought to myself,
"now all I need is a good weapon and I'll really be a force to be
reckoned with!" Set must have been listening, because on a shop on
level 6 there was the legendary long sword Frost Brand!!!

The only problem? A 16,000 zorkmid price tag. Ouch.

However, the priesthood of Set is both evil and chaotic, and I knew
that Set would not mind if Frost Brand were "liberated" from its owner
to benefit a righteous cause. I immediately resolved to be on the
lookout for anything in the dungeon that might help me steal the sword
while simultaneously avoiding the shopkeeper's wrath.

The Gnomish Mines didn't prove to be too much challenge (thanks
largely to my good equipment!) and I even ended up with a magic lamp
(which I decided to keep as a light source). But disaster struck on
mine level 6, when I encountered a hostile black (co-aligned) unicorn.
"It can't be real!" I thought, "Black unicorns are always friendly to
priests of Set! It must be some sort of shapeshifter or polymorphed
monster." Confident in this assumption, I struck down the wayward
unicorn, which turned out to be real somehow. (Maybe it counts as a
real unicorn if it's actually a polymorphed monster?) Set was not at
all happy about that little bit of sacrilege. Strangely, Set was also
not happy when I ate a dog. I couldn't remember any particular
religious scriptures regarding not eating dogs, but then I had never
been a very good student in seminary.

I ended up with a wand of teleport and a luckstone from the mines,
which was a good thing because Sokobon was pretty much a complete
disaster. This was partly due to my ineptitude with puzzles and partly
due to the bad luck of monsters spawning in the wrong place at the
wrong time. In short, Sokobon could only be solved by liberal
application of a pickaxe to many, many boulders, with corresponding
luck penalties. To add insult to injury, the haul from the Sokobon
treasure vault was considerably less than it could have been when a
rock mole ate a pile of 1,000+ gold pieces just before I was about to
grab it! The final treasure haul? A few hundred gold and a bag of
holding - a small consolation for all the effort to get that far.

The Battle of the Big Room (or, Wand of Teleport, Save Me!)

While delving further into the dungeon I fell down a trap door into an
immense cavern filled with monsters. This was the first serious combat
challenge that I had faced, and unfortunately, I was not up to the
task. I was only able to save myself by using both of the precious
charges from my wand of teleport to escape.

The Great Frost Brand Heist

Delving deeper I encountered my Quest and the Medusa's lair, but I was
not strong enough to face either of these challenges without a better
weapon than the unicorn horn I was using (a surprise encounter with a
rust monster had ended the career of my starting mace). Unfortunately,
planning for the Great Frost Brand Heist had hit a snag. Despite
exploring all of the Dungeon, Gnomish Mines, and Sokobon I had not
been able to find decent equipment for robbing the shop.

I hoped by appeasing Set I could gain His favor for the upcoming
robbery. I arranged for the "accidental" death of several fellow elves
and then sacrificed their corpses at altars to the apostate god Crom,
converting them to altars of Set. I even killed a heretical priestess
of Crom. Set must have been pleased because he sent the Demon Slime
Lord Juiblex to one of the altars. Or maybe Juiblex just showed up on
his own, I'm not really sure. Juiblex certainly didn't help me on my
mission any, though he seemed peaceful enough. When I went to chat
with him he merely gurgled, though I'm not sure what we could have
talked about in any case. What do you say to a putrescent slug-like
teleporting Demon Prince of Slime anyway? "Hey Juiblex, how's it
hanging today?" No, I think not.

The robbery plan, such as it was, relied on the time-honored military
tradition of "keep it simple, stupid." I would walk in the shop, pick
up the sword, zap a wand of digging downward, and exit smartly through
the floor, with the dumb shopkeeper being none the wiser. Certainly I
could have come up with something more elaborate if I had found better
tools to work with, but you go to thievery with the tools you have,
not the tools you want, yes?

But shopkeeper Yturaldi had a few tricks up his sleeve. Never
underestimate a shopkeeper! Sometimes I think they stay in business
not because of their overpriced goods, but because they end up
"inheriting" the equipment of all the foolish adventurers who try and
steal from them! When I carried out my plan Yturaldi AMAZINGLY leapt
across the large room (we're talking a significant distance here
folks) and grabbed my backpack! If "backpack grabbing" were a sport
he'd be the world champion! I fell through the hole I had made without
Frost Brand or the prime loot I had acquired through 20+ levels of
dangerous dungeon delving. Obviously, I don't rob shops much, or else
I might have seen this coming.

At first I had just wanted to get the sword peacefully, but now
Yturaldi had both the sword and my stuff! I had hoped to avoid
violence because I know that killing shopkeepers  means bad karma, but
now I really saw no choice. It was either Yturaldi or me, and I
resolved it wasn't going to be me.

I was afraid Yturaldi had a wand of death, or something similarly
lethal, so I tried to find either an item that gave reflection or my
own wand of death. Hoping for a wish, I sat on both opulent thrones I
found and drank from every fountain until they ran dry. I didn't get a
wish, though the watchmen in Gnome Town did get angry with me for
drinking from the town fountain. For their impertinence I locked them
in various buildings.

Little did I realize it, but my fountain-quaffing spree had set the
stage for a humiliating near-death experience. I got hungry and went
to eat some food from my inventory only to see I didn't have any. When
Yturaldi had taken my backpack he took my bag of holding with my best
food in it. When I had re-equipped myself with secondary gear from my
item stash I had carelessly neglected to include food in my new
inventory. Since I had been eating corpses I hadn't realized this
until now.

Now here's the best part. I was using a large box for my item stash. I
had a huge stockpile of more than 40 food rations in this box, but
when I went to open up the box I was unable to do so because I
discovered I was wielding a cursed two-handed weapon! Shocked, I
checked my inventory and sure enough, my unicorn horn was cursed, and
so was a bunch of other stuff in my inventory. Apparently drinking
from fountains is riskier than I thought!

So, as a priest my first thought was to use my abundant supply of holy
water to uncurse my unicorn horn - problem solved! Except, with
growing dread, I realized my substantial stash of potions of holy
water were either in my bag of holding (with Yturaldi)
or...in...my...box.

So, I decided to use the "remove curse" spell, which I knew. But
casting the spell exacerbated my hunger situation and in any event
proved impossible, as somehow my knowledge of the magic had become
twisted during my travels (100% fail rate). Of course I still had the
spellbook of remove curse, which I could use to re-learn the spell,
but it too was stored...in...my...inaccessible...box!

I was about to starve to death right on top of a mountain of food!!!

I went monster hunting, looking for corpses to eat, unsuccessfully.
When I started fainting I knew there was only one thing left to do:
Pray.

I just hoped my god had forgotten all about the unicorn-slaying
incident, the dog-eating incident, and the angered watchmen/shopkeeper
incidents. Otherwise, I was dead.

Thankfully Set was well-pleased! He made my stomach feel content and
even uncursed my unicorn horn! I was saved! Maybe my friend Juiblex
put in a good word for me with the man upstairs?

The war against Yturaldi took on new urgency. The loss of my luckstone
and bag of holding impeded any further dungeon delving. I needed my
stuff back, dammit! Since "Operation Get a Wish" hadn't got me
anything except nearly starved to death, I decided to put "Operation
Uber-Pet" into effect immediately.

Operation Uber-Pet

My plan was to acquire a pet, then lure it onto a polymorph trap until
it was permanently polymorphed into some badass monster, then have the
pet kill Yturaldi. This plan had the advantage of saving me from the
alignment hit that comes from killing shopkeepers since my pet was
doing the actual dirty work.

Complication: I had no pet. I'd entered the dungeon with a kitten but
I'd left it behind on the first dungeon level and it had gone feral in
my absence. I did however have a figurine of a gnome lord. I made the
long journey to the bottom of the Gnomish Mines where there were
polymorph traps only to find that my gnome lord was created peaceful
instead of tame. So much for my pet!

But all was not lost. I knew the "Find Familiar" spell. Sure, it was a
6th level spell and I knew it with a 92% failure rate, but it was one
of those "glass half-full or half-empty" deals. There was an 8% chance
of success! And with a wisdom of 20 I had no shortage of magical power
points to spare.

Many castings later I had my new pet kitten on a leash, and since it
was too smart to step on the trap I had to use the leash to forcibly
drag it across, snapping the leash in the process. The kitten became
polymorphed into an electric eel. Now, as we all know, eels are
aquatic creatures, so don't ask me how my kitten survived this
transformation. But I certainly wasn't going to complain. Electric
eels have a two turn instant death attack! My arch-nemesis Yturaldi
was toast!

I can only imagine what the fat shopkeeper would be thinking in the
last moments of his life. It would probably be something like, "Ha!
Look it's that dumb priest who tried to rob me! But what's that he's
got with him? An electric eel? On land? How this that possible?!
YEARGHHH!!! <insert dramatic death cry here>"

But my victory ruminations may have been a bit premature. It turns out
that while eels can survive on land, but they can't really move, they
can only flop around. Getting my uber-pet to the up-staircase was
going to be a hassle, let alone getting him all the way to Yturaldi's
shop. I hoped I could find a new leash to help speed up the movement
process. But all my plans came to nothing when a winter wolf appeared
and, with one freezing breath, shattered my hopes and dreams for my
uber-pet. Shattered my uber-pet too.

Apparently polymorphed pets keep their original hit points? Or maybe
electric eels are weaker than I thought. In any case, that was the end
of Operation Uber-Pet and I was no closer to solving the Yturaldi
problem. At a loss about what to do, I started using the wands of
create monster I had found to generate sacrificial fodder for an altar
of Set.

One of the monsters created was a cockatrice. Which created a corpse
when killed. Hot. Damn. Suddenly a new anti-Yturaldi plan presented
itself.

I still had my gauntlets of ogre power, so I carefully picked up the
creature's corpse. Engaging Yturaldi in melee combat was terribly
risky, but I was out of options at this point.

Hoping that Yturaldi didn't have a wand of death I boldly strode up to
him and whacked him with the cockatrice corpse. A hit! Yturaldi
started to turn to stone! Vengeance was mine! Or not.

Yturaldi ate a lizard corpse. MY lizard corpse. It had been in my
backpack when Yturaldi had stolen it. Now I was really ticked off. He
was using my own stuff against me! Would my stolen luckstone also
grant luck to the shopkeeper? I hoped not. There was nothing left to
do except keep hitting Yturaldi with the cockatrice until he ran out
of lizards or I missed and he zapped me with an offensive wand, which
would probably be the death of me.

Screaming, "Give me my stuff back!!!" I pressed the attack. And won.

Victory!

I smashed the shopkeeper's sneering statue with my pickaxe and
recovered my stuff, as well as the great Frost Brand, which I
immediately rust-proofed and enchanted to +5. As a priest I was
unskilled in the use of the weapon, but that hardly mattered since it
was enchanted so much.

With my new weapon I stormed Medusa's lair and got a nice shield of
reflection for my trouble. While besieging the castle on the next
level I encountered a dreaded disenchanter. I had to use a ring of
conflict to let others kill it, and then shortly thereafter I blessed
a scroll of genocide to get rid the highly annoying rust monsters and
disenchanters. I also genocided vampires for good measure since their
level draining attacks had already cost me a few levels. I had one
blessed scroll of genocide left but I decided to keep it for an
emergency.

Thanks to my earlier fountain quaffing spree I now had near maximum
stats. This came in handy when I encountered a succubus. Usually I got
burned in my dealings with these evil seductresses, so I killed them
from a distance before they could use their charms on me. But I had
heard rumors of adventurers who, with good stats, had been able to
play succubuses at their own game and come out ahead. I had never
tried it before, but after breezing through Medusa and the castle I
was feeling adventurous. I let the succubus seduce me and most of the
time I did come out ahead! I think I'd have gotten a lot of stat
increases except my stats were already at the max. I did however go
from experience level 16 to level 21. Wow!!! If I ever did make it
back to my beloved elf enchantress on the surface I'd have lots of
tricks to show her!

But after I while I felt kind of cheesy for exploiting the succubus
and I put her out of her misery. I also resolved never to do it again
(too cheesy to get 5 levels from one monster).

I found an uncursed amulet and like all uncursed items I put it on to
see what it would do. Oops! An amulet of changing! I went from being a
Lama of Set to being a Matriarch of Set. I hoped my beloved elf
enchantress would understand.

I did my Quest, but my Quest nemesis, Nalzok, turned out to be a
complete wuss. At first I thought I was in trouble when he started
summoning powerful monsters to surround me. But three hits from my
enchanted Frost Brand later the demon had fallen and the Mitre of
Holiness was mine.

My item stash had increased in size tremendously. I now had over 40
potions of holy water and dozens of wands stuffed in my large box. I
used all my scrolls of enchant armor to harden my defense and when I
got a wish from a djinni in a smoky bottle I wished for a blessed
fireproof +2 T-shirt. Now my unbuffed armor class was -28 and I felt
ready to take on the Land of the Dead and whatever lay beyond it.

But during my descent through the Dungeon I stumbled upon a portal to
a little place called Fort Ludios. The Yendoran Army was about to have
a VERY bad day. The unfortunate soldiers never stood much of a chance
against Frost Brand and my -28 AC, though to their credit they fought
to the last. I ended up with a massive haul of money butby this point
there was nothing left in the shops worth buying. I purchased some
advice from the Oracle for a rather hefty fee but she had no new
insight to give me and I felt kind of cheated.

I cleaved through the Valley of Death, though I'm sure my progress was
made easier because all liches and vampires had been genocided.
Gehennom proved to be surprisingly easy as well. The demon princes
threw me off guard for a while, until I caught on to their staircase-
teleporting tricks. I cut through Vlad and his minions easily and
found an amulet of life saving, which I immediately put on. Aside from
an accident with a potion of polymorph that resulted in the
destruction of a valuable cloak of displacement I was doing great. I
had identified all items in the game by this point. I was pumped up
with excitement and could taste my imminent victory.

Trust the Wizard of Yendor to show up and ruin my day. Sure, I
defeated the Wizard's minions easily. The Wizard himself also seemed
an easy kill. I eagerly searched his corpse, expecting to find the
Amulet only to find...some dumb book? What???

I had hit the bottom of Gehennom. I'd completely searched every
dungeon level and dungeon branch. There was no Amulet of Yendor
anywhere. Worse, the Wizard of Yendor did not stay dead! He came back
much tougher than before and now he was using the same stair-
teleporting trick the demon princes had used earlier. As I retraced my
steps through Gehenom looking for any possible Amulet hiding place I'd
missed the Wizard constantly harrassed me, cursed my items or outright
stealing them. It was the item cursing that worried me the most. My
items were being cursed faster than I could uncurse them. I was
running out of holy water to uncurse them in any case. I was on
dungeon level 50 and I needed to get back to my stash on dungeon level
11 before all my inventory was cursed or stolen.

So began a mad dash upward, the Wizard harrying me the whole time.
Disaster struck when the wizard stole my helmet and a master mind
flayer took advantage of this to try and separate me from my brain.
The intelligence loss was easily rectified by a potion of restore
ability, but much more seriously I lost knowledge of many dungeon
levels and most of the equipment I had identified. One of the few
scrolls that I still remembered the function of was my blessed scroll
of genocide. Guess what monster class I decided to genocide?

It was during this darkest hour when I first died. I was wielding a
cockatrice corpse, whacking anyone who got in my way, especially that
damn Wizard. But I fell into a pit and was petrified by my own weapon.
Luckily I was wearing the amulet of life saving and even more luckily
the Wizard hadn't managed to curse it yet.

Note to self: Do not wield cockatrice corpses if there might be pit
traps in the area!

So I lived but lost the amulet. I re-equipped myself at my stash but
had no idea where to go or what to do. I paid the Oracle an outrageous
1800 zorkmids for another major consultation but once again she was no
help. Meanwhile the Wizard's persistent attacks were eroding my
resources much faster than I could replace them.

With no other option, I turned to the font of knowledge that some call
"the Internet." While some might consider consulting the Internet to
be spoilerish or cheesy, like I said, I was out of options at that
point.

And so I learned of the invocation ritual, and made the long trek back
down to the depths of Gehennom and invaded the Sanctum. In the Sanctum
no less than three wands of death were used against me but my shield
of reflection saved the day and I ended up with the wands and the
Amulet. Huzzah!!!

Even better, I found the Wizard was vulnerable to wands of death. He
died. A lot.

I ascended against surprisingly little opposition. I was worried about
my encumbrance level so I dropped all non-essential gear, left
150,000+ gold pieces in my stash, grabbed all my gems, and headed for
the surface. I saw my now-feral kitten on dungeon level 1 where I had
abandoned her so long ago. I ended up on the Planes.

I accidentally found the portal out of the Plane of Earth after taking
just a few steps. The Plane of Air was another matter however. It was
pure chance that I still had my boots of levitation with me. I had
only taken them with me to cross the water at Medusa's lair, the
castle and the swamp, and I'd forgotten to drop them off at my stash
with all my other supposedly nonessential stuff.

Yet without those boots I'm sure I would have died. Or at least, died
more than I did. Hostile Angels, Archons, Ki-rins, and Titans and
other despicable do-gooders summoned swarms of powerful monsters to
surround me turn after turn, much faster than I could kill them.  I'd
get out of one pack of dragons only to have two more summoned around
me.  This wasn't challenging combat. The Big Room was challenging
combat. This was ludicrous insane suicidal combat.  I quickly realized
that the only way for me to survive the hordes was to wear my ring of
conflict and make everybody fight each other. I put on the ring and
immediately the Nethack equivalent of World War 3 broke out.

This was by far the most intense combat I'd seen yet, or that I'd even
known was possible. There were dragons breathing rays of
disintegration at other dragons. Soldiers zapping offensive wands at
anyone in sight. Titans wielding cockatrice corpses against all
comers. It was an all-out brawl.

Twice I got stoned by cockatrice corpse-wielding enemies faster than I
could eat a lizard. Both times I was saved by amulets of life saving.
My "ace in the hole" was the wand of wishing I'd gotten from the
castle. I used all three charges on amulets of life saving. Even so, I
was afraid I was going to run out of amulets. I spend most of my time
just trying to stay alive by staying out of combat (the edges of the
map or better yet the corners were nice hiding spots, as only a few
enemies could attack me at those locations). But my angelic opponents
seemed immune to my ring of conflict and continued to summon monster
hordes around me. I had heard that if 120 monsters of a specific type
were killed then no more monsters of that type will ever be generated.
I felt certain the only way I would survive the onslaught would be for
the nastiest monsters to become extinct.

Eventually though, I stumbled through the portal. What a relief! The
next two Planes weren't as bad and then I stood on the Astral Plane
with one amulet of life saving left. I fought my way to the altar of
Set, guarded by Pestilence. Pestilence sickened me and I knew I had
only a few turns to live. I dropped the Amulet on the altar and
sacrificed it.

"Nothing happens" was the response.

I couldn't believe it!

Had I come all this way only to die here at the very end, my goal in
sight?

I thought perhaps it hadn't worked because the Amulet was cursed. I
picked up the Amulet and uncursed it. Pestilence killed me, but my
amulet of life saving staved off death. I was quickly running out of
such amulets though. I dropped the Amulet again. Pestilence sickened
me again. I sacrificed the Amulet again.

"Nothing happens" again.

I nearly lost my mind!

Had the Wizard of Yendor somehow switched out my real Amulet for one
of his fake ones? Was the real Amulet lying in a dark crevice
somewhere around dungeon level 50, far beyond my reach? Despair filled
me. But no, I had used a scroll of identify to positively identify the
Amulet when I had reached my stash on dungeon level 11. It had to be
real! So what was I doing wrong? Pestilence was about to kill me, and
death would be final this time!

I tried offering the Amulet without dropping it on the altar first.

I ascended. Victory!

----------------------------
I killed 5777 monsters including the Wizard of Yendor x17, Asmodeus,
Orcus, Balzebub, Famine x2, and Pestilence. I used 6 wishes (mostly
for amulets of life saving). I was experience level 28. I genocided
rust monsters, disenchanters, vampires, liches, and humanoids (my
specific target was mind flayers but dwarves, bugbears and hobbits
ended up being collateral damage).

Prior to this game I had never made it past the first level of
Gehennom. So I was familiar with the Dungeon, Gnomish Mines, and
Sokobon but I knew little of the Medusa, Castle, Fort Ludios, or
Valley of the Dead and nothing about Gehennom or anything after that.


